<p>Two claims dominate the advertising of hair restoration in India: a success rate somewhere in the high nineties, and a guarantee sitting beside it. Both are meant to read as evidence. Neither behaves like evidence when you press on it, and the more precise the figure, the less likely anyone can say where it came from.</p>

<p>What follows is about the claims and the rules that govern them, not about any particular clinic, and about what a clinic can honestly commit to in place of a number.</p>

<h2 id="what-a-survival-percentage-would-have-to-measure">What a graft survival percentage would have to measure</h2>

<p>Suppose a clinic genuinely wanted to publish one. It would first have to decide what is counted. Follicular units are transplanted, but hairs are what you see, and a unit may carry one to four hairs. Surviving hairs and surviving units give different answers from the same scalp.</p>

<p>Then the method. The only defensible one is a marked reference area of fixed size, photographed under identical magnification and lighting before surgery and again afterwards, with hairs counted rather than estimated. That belongs to a research setting, not a busy clinic.</p>

<p>A further problem: the recipient area holds native hairs as well as transplanted ones, so unless those were counted at baseline, a higher count cannot be attributed to the grafts — particularly as pattern loss continues underneath, which is <a href="/blog/is-a-hair-transplant-permanent">why a transplant does not stop hair loss</a>. Androgenetic alopecia is progressive and surgery does not switch it off (<a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/male-pattern-hair-loss" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet on male pattern hair loss</a>).</p>

<h3>The interval</h3>

<p>A count at three months is meaningless and one at six understates the result, because transplanted hairs shed and then regrow on their own schedule. Any honest figure would have to state its interval, and it would have to be ten to twelve months — which is why <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-growth-timeline-month-by-month">the month-by-month growth timeline</a> is worth reading before you judge anything.</p>

<h3>Who was counted</h3>

<p>A rate is only as good as its denominator. A figure drawn from patients who came back at twelve months excludes everyone who did not, and the least satisfied are the least likely to return. That measures not outcomes, but the outcomes of people willing to be photographed.</p>

<h2 id="why-nobody-can-audit-your-outcome-in-advance">Why nobody can audit your outcome in advance</h2>

<p>Set aside whether the number was honestly derived. Even a rigorous figure describes past patients, not you, because the variables deciding your result are individual and largely invisible until someone examines you.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Donor supply and quality.</strong> Measured density, calibre and scalp laxity differ enormously between people and set the ceiling on what is possible — the constraint behind <a href="/blog/donor-area-limits-hair-transplant">what a donor area can realistically fund</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Whether the loss has stabilised.</strong> Operating into actively progressing loss gives a result that looks good at a year and poor at five, however well the grafts took.</li>
  <li><strong>Recipient-area condition.</strong> Scarring, previous surgery, scalp disease and blood supply all affect how a graft settles.</li>
  <li><strong>Health and healing.</strong> Uncontrolled diabetes, smoking and certain medicines change wound healing, and some are reasons <a href="/blog/who-should-not-have-a-hair-transplant">surgery is sometimes the wrong answer</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of that is knowable from a photograph sent on WhatsApp. Some becomes knowable after an examination, and even then the honest output is a range of coverage, not a percentage.</p>

<h2 id="the-law-that-names-baldness">The law that names baldness by name</h2>

<p>India has legislated on this exact subject since 1954. The Drugs and Magic Remedies (Objectionable Advertisements) Act prohibits advertisements suggesting that a drug or remedy will bring about the cure, mitigation, treatment or prevention of any condition listed in its Schedule. Baldness sits on that Schedule. A separate provision prohibits advertisements making false claims about a drug or exaggerating its effect.</p>

<p>The Act targets drugs and remedies rather than surgical technique, and how far it reaches a surgical advertisement is a question for lawyers. The intent is not in doubt: Parliament identified hair loss, seventy years ago, as a field where cure claims would be made to people inclined to believe them.</p>

<h2 id="what-the-advertising-code-says">What the advertising code says about superlatives and testimonials</h2>

<p>The Advertising Standards Council of India runs the self-regulatory code for advertising across media, and its healthcare guidance is stricter still. Three points matter here.</p>

<p><strong>Claims must be substantiable before publication.</strong> The advertiser is expected to hold the evidence when the claim is made, not assemble a justification after a complaint. A survival percentage with no stated method, interval or sample is exactly what the code treats as unsupported.</p>

<p><strong>Superlatives need proof.</strong> "Best", "number one" and "most advanced" are comparative claims, and a comparative claim requires evidence of the comparison. Such wording appears mainly where no comparison was ever measured.</p>

<p><strong>A testimonial cannot carry a claim the clinic could not make directly.</strong> A patient saying on camera that the outcome was guaranteed does not launder an unsubstantiated claim through someone else's mouth. Worth remembering when you assess a video wall, alongside <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">how to read a before-and-after photograph</a>.</p>

<h2 id="why-results-may-vary-repairs-nothing">Why "results may vary" repairs nothing</h2>

<p>The usual fix for an overstated claim is a line of small print beneath it, and it does not work. The Central Consumer Protection Authority's 2022 guidelines on misleading advertisements are explicit that a disclaimer may not contradict, correct or conceal the material claim of an advertisement. A disclaimer can add detail. It cannot rescue a headline that was not true.</p>

<blockquote>If the headline says ninety-eight per cent and the footnote says results vary between individuals, the two are in conflict. The footnote is not a qualification of the claim but an admission that it could not be supported.</blockquote>

<p>The same applies to "guaranteed results, terms and conditions apply". Either the outcome is guaranteed, and the terms say what happens when it is not, or it is not, and the word should not appear.</p>

<h2 id="what-a-clinic-can-honestly-commit-to">What a clinic can honestly commit to</h2>

<p>Declining to quote a survival rate is not the same as promising nothing. Much can be committed to in writing, because it is within the clinic's control.</p>

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr><th scope="col">Cannot honestly be promised</th><th scope="col">Can be put in writing</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>A graft survival percentage</td><td>Which doctor performs each stage</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A guaranteed density or coverage</td><td>Planned graft number, areas treated, reasoning</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A fixed number of years before further loss</td><td>A named review schedule with dated photographs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>That no further session will be needed</td><td>An itemised estimate agreed before the day</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Who operates is the first and least often specified. India's medical regulator is clear about which qualifications may perform this surgery, so asking in writing is fair — see <a href="/blog/who-can-legally-perform-a-hair-transplant-in-india">who is legally permitted to perform the surgery</a>. At DenceSpot it is performed by <a href="/dr-nyra">the dermatologist who runs the clinic</a>, a commitment about staffing rather than outcomes. The written estimate is the second: a plan that survives a printed page is a plan; a figure existing only in conversation is not. How one is built is set out on <a href="/hair-transplant-cost-in-gurgaon">the page explaining what a hair transplant costs</a>, with payment arrangements on <a href="/cost-and-emi-options">the cost and EMI options page</a>.</p>

