<h2 id="how-to-respond-when-a-guarantee-is-offered">How to respond when a guarantee is offered</h2>

<p>You will not change anyone's marketing in a consultation room, and there is no need to argue. Ask ordinary, specific questions, then listen to how they are answered.</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>What does the percentage measure.</strong> A reasonable answer names hairs or units, an interval and a counting method. An unreasonable one restates the number more confidently.</li>
  <li><strong>How many patients was it calculated from, and how many returned at twelve months.</strong> The follow-up rate is the part nobody volunteers.</li>
  <li><strong>What exactly is guaranteed, and what happens if it is not achieved.</strong> If the answer is a repeat session, ask who decides that the threshold was missed, and by what measurement.</li>
  <li><strong>What in my case could make the result worse than average.</strong> Every candidate has something. A clinic unwilling to name yours has either not examined you properly or has decided not to say.</li>
</ol>

<p>None of these is confrontational. They are what a careful person asks about any significant purchase, and they overlap with the wider list of <a href="/blog/questions-to-ask-hair-transplant-consultation">questions worth asking at a consultation</a>. A clinic that welcomes them tells you something. So does one that becomes impatient.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-ask-for-instead-of-a-number">What to ask for instead of a number</h2>

<p><strong>The examination findings.</strong> Ask what was measured, not what was concluded: donor density, the extent of miniaturisation, the areas mapped, whether the loss appears active. Observations from two clinics can be compared in a way two graft quotes cannot, which is much of <a href="/blog/why-hair-transplant-quotes-vary">why two quotes for the same head differ</a>.</p>

<p><strong>The stated limits.</strong> Ask what the plan will not achieve. A frontal-third restoration leaving the crown untouched is legitimate, but you should hear it described that way before consenting, not discover it at month twelve. Ask too what the first weeks look like, because <a href="/blog/shedding-and-shock-loss-after-hair-transplant">shock loss and early shedding</a> alarm people who were not warned.</p>

<p><strong>The reasoning.</strong> Ask why this plan and not another: why this many grafts, why this area first, why now rather than after a course of medical treatment. Reasoning can be examined by a second doctor; a number cannot. Take the answers away, write them down, and see whether they still hold together a week later without anyone in the room to defend them. The overview of <a href="/hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">hair transplant surgery in Gurugram</a> sets out that sequence, and the <a href="/hair-transplant-results-gurgaon">documented case photographs</a> carry stated intervals rather than adjectives.</p>

<p>None of this is an argument against surgery. Hair transplantation is well established, and for the right candidate it does what it is meant to do. But no treatment for hair loss carries an assured outcome, and health services describing these treatments say so plainly (<a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hair-loss/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS on hair loss</a>). A clinic that says that, then explains what it can control instead, gives you more than one offering a certainty nobody can deliver.</p>

